.TH IAD\-RESPONSIVE\-TABLE 3 "" "iced-adaptive-design 0.1.0" "iced-adaptive-design Manual"
.SH NAME
responsive_table \- Rows of T as a table or as cards, by the width of the container
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
ResponsiveTable::new(rows: &'a [T])
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
Rows of \fBT\fR as a table or as cards, by the width of the container. See the module.
.PP
\fBResponsiveTable\fR: one dataset, two presentations. A table while the columns fit, a stack of cards when they would not.
.PP
A table with many columns usually ends up scrolling on the X axis, which hides the cells that matter. Here the *container* (not the window) decides: when its width is below what the columns need (\fBColumn::min_width\fR) every row is rendered as a card instead, with a custom layout if you give one (\fBResponsiveTable::card\fR) and a label/value list otherwise. Nothing is ever clipped or scrolled sideways.
.PP
.nf
.RS
ResponsiveTable::new(&self.people)
    .column(Column::new("Name", |p: &Person| body(&p.name).into()).min_width(140.0))
    .column(Column::new("Role", |p: &Person| body(&p.role).into()).min_width(120.0))
    .column(Column::new("Email", |p: &Person| body(&p.email).into()).min_width(220.0))
    // optional: your own card layout
    .card(|p| column![heading(&p.name), caption(&p.email)].into())
.RE
.fi
.PP
\fBSorting.\fR Mark columns with \fBColumn::sortable\fR\fB(key)\fR, pass the app's \fBSortState\fR to \fBResponsiveTable::sort\fR and handle \fBResponsiveTable::on_sort\fR\fB(key)\fR by calling \fBstate.activate(key, false)\fR (ascending, descending, unsorted; see \fBprimitives::sorting\fR) and sorting the rows with \fBstate.apply\fR. In the table the header cell is a button with an arrow (none, up, down; a number when several columns sort); the cards presentation has no header row, so the same events come from a \fBsort control above the cards\fR: a dropdown of the sortable columns and a direction button. Both send the same \fBon_sort(key)\fR, so the app has one code path. Sorting the data and paging it are the app's job; \fBResponsiveTable::footer\fR is where a \fBPagination\fR goes.
.PP
\fBVirtualization.\fR A table of thousands of rows builds only the ones in view: \fBvirtualized\fR\fB(total, first)\fR says the rows you pass are \fBfirst..first + rows.len()\fR of \fBtotal\fR rows of a fixed height (set by \fBdensity\fR, or \fBrow_height\fR), and the table pads the scrolled area above and below with exact spacers so the scrollbar is true to the whole list. Feed it from \fBon_scroll\fR: keep a \fBViewport\fR, ask it for the \fBwindow\fR and pass that slice. Rows that are fetched as the list is scrolled are \fBChunks\fR: a row whose chunk has not arrived is a placeholder your cell closures draw, a failed one an error row with Retry. Cards have no fixed height, so a virtualized table is always a table.
.PP
\fBA plain table.\fR With \fBMode::Table\fR it is a table at any width: the columns \fBshrink\fR (every column grows by default, shares the free width, and clips what does not fit instead of wrapping or overflowing), \fBColumn::width\fR fixes one, \fBColumn::align\fR right\-aligns a time or a count, and \fBdensity\fR picks roomy or compact rows. A music, podcast or file list is this: a picture\-and\-title column, an album column, a fixed time column, virtualized, sortable.
.PP
\fBResponsiveTable\fR is a \fBMetaComponent\fR with two variants, a table and cards; the choice is \fBResponsiveTable::when\fR. Like \fBresponsive\fR, it fills its container and scrolls vertically by itself; give it a bounded height.
